{"title":"datasette-charts 0.3a0","link":"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/25/datasette-charts/","date":1795630610000,"content":"\n    \n        <p><strong>Release:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/datasette/datasette-charts/releases/tag/0.3a0\">datasette-charts 0.3a0</a></p>\n        <p>I added chart annotations to <a href=\"https://github.com/datasette/datasette-charts\">datasette-charts</a>!</p>\n<p>You can now render a chart and then annotate it with lines, shaded areas, labels, or a combination of the three.</p>\n<p>A chart with annotations looks like this:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Screenshot of an annotated bar chart with the y-axis label &quot;↑ Total capacity_mw&quot;, showing blue bars for each decade from 1950 to 2010. Capacity is low in the earlier decades, then jumps sharply for 2000 and 2010. The chart carries three annotations: a gray shaded band across 1950 to 1990 with the note &quot;Much smaller summed capacity in earlier decades&quot;, a dot on top of the 2000 bar labeled &quot;Largest decade total in these records: 1.157 million MW&quot;, and a dashed horizontal line labeled &quot;1,000,000 MW reference&quot;. A caption below reads &quot;Global plant records with commissioning years 1950–2019; coverage and missing years may affect comparisons.&quot;\" src=\"/media/d3d24b64936a1df79373859ce0395d3e6b52f99b8f73fad0f50dd2afecd8434a.webp\" /></p>\n<p>Here's <a href=\"https://lite.datasette.io/?install=datasette-charts&amp;url=https://datasette.io/global-power-plants.db#/global-power-plants/-/query?sql=SELECT+CAST%28commissioning_year+%2F+10+AS+INTEGER%29+%2A+10+AS+decade%2C+capacity_mw+FROM+%22global-power-plants%22+WHERE+commissioning_year+BETWEEN+1950+AND+2019+AND+capacity_mw+IS+NOT+NULL#chart=%7B%22v%22%3A1%2C%22type%22%3A%22bar%22%2C%22encoding%22%3A%7B%22x%22%3A%7B%22column%22%3A%22decade%22%2C%22sort%22%3A%22key%22%2C%22limit%22%3A20%2C%22other%22%3Afalse%7D%2C%22y%22%3A%7B%22column%22%3A%22capacity_mw%22%2C%22aggregate%22%3A%22sum%22%7D%7D%2C%22options%22%3A%7B%22orientation%22%3A%22vertical%22%7D%2C%22annotations%22%3A%5B%7B%22type%22%3A%22point%22%2C%22x%22%3A2000%2C%22text%22%3A%22Largest%20decade%20total%20in%20these%20records%3A%201.157%20million%20MW%22%7D%2C%7B%22type%22%3A%22range%22%2C%22x%22%3A1950%2C%22x2%22%3A1990%2C%22text%22%3A%22Much%20smaller%20summed%20capacity%20in%20earlier%20decades%22%7D%2C%7B%22type%22%3A%22line%22%2C%22y%22%3A1000000%2C%22text%22%3A%221%2C000%2C000%20MW%20reference%22%7D%2C%7B%22type%22%3A%22text%22%2C%22left%22%3A0.02%2C%22top%22%3A0.04%2C%22text%22%3A%22Global%20plant%20records%20with%20commissioning%20years%201950%E2%80%932019%3B%20coverage%20and%20missing%20years%20may%20affect%20comparisons.%22%7D%5D%7D\">that same chart in Datasette Lite</a>, where you can tweak the annotations using the chart builder UI:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Animated screen recording of an &quot;Edit annotation&quot; dialog for a chart titled &quot;Total capacity_mw by decade&quot;. A form on the left has fields for &quot;Kind&quot;, &quot;Capacity_mw&quot;, &quot;Label (optional)&quot;, &quot;Label position&quot;, &quot;Color&quot; and &quot;Line style&quot;, and a &quot;PREVIEW&quot; panel on the right shows a blue bar chart of total capacity by decade from 1950 to 2010, with a dashed horizontal line labeled &quot;1,000,000 MW reference&quot;. The preview updates live as the user edits: changing the Capacity_mw value from &quot;1000000&quot; to &quot;900000&quot; and then &quot;800000&quot; moves the dashed line down the chart in step, and editing the label text, first adding an exclamation mark, then removing it and deleting the word &quot;reference&quot;, changes the label on the line to &quot;1,000,000 MW reference!&quot; and finally &quot;1,000,000 MW&quot;. The user then clicks the &quot;Save&quot; button.\" src=\"/media/6e55d3ea96ad316dca6db4de53ba1dbcc897d210896170cc4ab81400442864fd.gif\" /></p>\n<p>If you also install <a href=\"https://agent.datasette.io/\">Datasette Agent</a> the agent can build annotated charts for you. Here's what I got on the <a href=\"https://agent.datasette.io/\">agent.datasette.io</a> demo for this prompt:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>Show a chart comparing Simon's tagged content about LLMs with his tagged content about Datasette, with annotations</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><img alt=\"Screenshot of a chat-style AI agent interface that has produced a chart in response to a question. At the top is a collapsed reasoning line reading &quot;Thinking: …26 partial year is labeled correctly in the output. Let's go with this approach!&quot; followed by a collapsed tool call labeled &quot;chart&quot;. Below that is a line chart titled &quot;Total content_count per year by topic&quot;, with a legend for &quot;LLMs&quot; (blue) and &quot;Datasette&quot; (orange), the y-axis label &quot;↑ Total content_count&quot; and years 2017 to 2026 on the x-axis. The Datasette line stays low throughout, with an annotation &quot;Datasette peak year&quot; pointing at 2020. The LLMs line sits near zero until 2022, then climbs steeply to a peak in 2025 before dropping in 2026, with an annotation &quot;LLM-tagged content rises&quot; pointing at 2023. A note on the chart reads &quot;Counts include posts, notes and links; 2026 is year-to-date.&quot; Under the chart are the controls &quot;Open in the chart builder&quot;, &quot;Copy link&quot;, &quot;This chart's SQL&quot;, &quot;Copy TSV&quot; and &quot;Copy CSV&quot;. Another collapsed reasoning line reads &quot;Thinking: …rends start low for LLMs in 2020, and they're projected to rise sharply by 2026.&quot; The agent's reply then says: &quot;The chart compares annual counts of non-draft posts, notes, and link posts tagged with LLM-related terms or Datasette-related terms. The annotations mark Datasette's peak year (2020) and the sharp rise in LLM-tagged content beginning in 2023. 2026 is year-to-date.&quot; At the bottom is a message box with the placeholder &quot;Type a message... (# to tag a table or query)&quot; and a &quot;Send&quot; button.\" src=\"/media/4d41d48ae3667ede28773b618a2d70d5f79e1e8d35485defae136c08970cf5bf.webp\" /></p>\n    \n    \n        <p>Tags: <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/datasette\">datasette</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/visualization\">visualization</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/datasette-charts\">datasette-charts</a></p>\n    \n\n","author":"Simon Willison","siteTitle":"Simon Willison’s Weblog","siteHash":"1b599216bd27a00f15d67a3ed9719c64148630c70244b6dd8e743f17cb45527e","entryHash":"3df59a96b909102579912461d415833cec461fc04d5dbd53b1c0047795f45667","category":"Tech"}